    howItWorks:
      "New York City is made up of five boroughs, each of which is also a county of New York State: Manhattan, Brooklyn, Queens, the Bronx and Staten Island. The city is governed by a mayor and a 51-member City Council, each borough has its own president, and beneath them lie 59 community districts; yet the subway and commuter railways New Yorkers depend on belong to the Metropolitan Transportation Authority, run by the state rather than the city. The 2020 census counted about 8.58 million people in the city in 2025 — down from 8.8 million at the 2020 census — and about 19.9 million in a metropolitan area reaching into New Jersey, Long Island and Connecticut. Manhattan is a grid of numbered streets and avenues, while the outer boroughs keep street systems of their own; in local speech 'the city' means Manhattan alone. Daily range is set by the subway line, and the end of a line is often the edge of a neighbourhood. Newcomers most often go wrong by equating New York with Manhattan, when four of the five boroughs lie off the island and hold the great majority of New Yorkers.",
