    //   https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/anchoragemunicipalityalaska/PST045225（1 级，2026-09-08 核）。
    identity:
      "Anchorage is Alaska's largest city, between the Knik and Turnagain arms of Cook Inlet, with about 287,000 people (2025 estimate, down from 291,000 at the 2020 census) — still nearly 40 percent of the state. It began in 1914 as a construction camp for the Alaska Railroad, and the magnitude 9.2 Good Friday earthquake of 27 March 1964, the second-strongest ever recorded, devastated the city. Anchorage is the gateway to the Alaskan wilderness — the Chugach Mountains and Chugach State Park press against its eastern edge, Denali lies to the north, and Lake Hood is the busiest seaplane base in the world; daylight lasts nearly 19 hours around the summer solstice and about 6 hours at the winter solstice.",
    // howItWorks sources: en.wikipedia (2026-09, Tier B short): Anchorage (Alaska; a unified home rule municipality merging city and borough since 1975, about 1,946 sq mi, less than 10 per cent of it populated; 291,247 in 2020, nearly 40 per cent of Alaska; founded 1914 as a railroad construction camp, incorporated 1920; on a peninsula between the Knik and Turnagain arms of Cook Inlet, Chugach Mountains behind; magnitude 9.2 earthquake in 1964; Ted Stevens airport the world's third-busiest for cargo; about 250 black bears and 1,000 moose; Juneau is the state capital)
    howItWorks:
      "Anchorage is Alaska's largest city, a unified home rule municipality merging city and borough since 1975 covering about 5,000 km², less than a tenth of it populated; it had about 287,000 people in the 2025 estimate, close to forty per cent of the state. It appeared in 1914 as a construction camp for the Alaska Railroad and was incorporated in 1920, a city wholly of the twentieth century. The town lies on a peninsula between two arms of Cook Inlet with the Chugach Mountains behind, and the magnitude 9.2 earthquake of 1964 — the second strongest ever recorded — dropped a block of the city into the sea, where a park now stands. Ted Stevens airport is one of the world's busiest cargo airports, where the great circle routes between Asia and North America refuel; the Alaska Railroad has its terminus in town and the Iditarod sled dog race starts ceremonially from the downtown streets each March. About a thousand moose come into the city in winter and some two hundred and fifty black bears live within it. The state capital is not here but at Juneau to the south. Newcomers most often go wrong by assuming Anchorage is the capital, when Juneau is, and can be reached only by boat or plane.",
    layout:
      "Downtown sits on a terrace on the south shore of Knik Arm in a street grid, with the Anchorage Museum, the weekend market and the railroad depot all in the centre; the Tony Knowles Coastal Trail runs about 18 km south-west along the shore from downtown to Kincaid Park. The airport and Lake Hood lie south-west of downtown, and the Alaska Native Heritage Center in the north-east. The Chugach Mountains rise to the east, with Flattop the most-climbed peak; Turnagain Arm runs south along the Seward Highway — the stretch for beluga whales and the bore tide — with the Alyeska ski resort about 60 km south at Girdwood. Denali National Park is about 380 km north.",
    gettingAround:
      "Ted Stevens Anchorage International Airport (ANC), about 8 km from downtown, has flights to the major US cities and is a leading air-cargo hub. The Alaska Railroad runs from Anchorage north to Denali and Fairbanks and south to Seward and Whittier, daily in summer — the classic way to travel. The city has buses; downtown is walkable and the coastal trail good for cycling; the wilderness needs a car or a tour, and sightseeing flights leave from Lake Hood. The climate is subarctic: 12–20°C in summer, −15 to −5°C in winter with deep snow.",
    culture:
      "English is universal, and the Dena'ina Athabascan people are the original inhabitants of this land; the Alaska Native Heritage Center presents the cultures of the whole state. Wildlife can appear anywhere: moose are common in city parks — keep your distance — and in bear country carry bear spray, go in groups and make noise. Tip 15–20% in restaurants. Downtown is crowded in the summer cruise season; winter brings sled-dog races (Fur Rondy and the ceremonial start of the Iditarod). The aurora can be seen on clear nights from September to April away from city lights.",
    seeAndDo:
      "The Tony Knowles Coastal Trail (cycling, moose and distant Denali); the Anchorage Museum; the Alaska Native Heritage Center; floatplanes at Lake Hood and the Alaska Aviation Museum; the Flattop Mountain hike; the Turnagain Arm drive for belugas and the bore tide, and the Alaska Wildlife Conservation Center; the Alyeska tram at Girdwood; the train or boat to Seward for the glaciers of Kenai Fjords; flightseeing to Denali or the glaciers; the summer weekend market.",
    whenAndTips:
      "June–August is the peak, with the longest days and every activity running — book lodging and trains early; September brings autumn colour and the first auroras with fewer people; winter is severe but offers sledding and auroras. Two days for Anchorage itself, as the base for Denali, the Kenai Peninsula and the Matanuska Glacier. Mosquitoes are thick in summer — bring repellent.",
