    //   1,261,337 / 第 47 两说），没拿到普查局当期都会区排名表，按红线不写具体名次。
    identity:
      "Salt Lake City is the capital of Utah, at the western foot of the Wasatch Range south-east of the Great Salt Lake, at about 1,300 m, with about 218,000 people (2025 estimate) and a metro area of about 1.3 million. It was founded in 1847 by pioneers of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints led by Brigham Young; Temple Square is the origin point of the city grid and the church's headquarters. The city hosted the 2002 Winter Olympics and in 2024 was chosen to host the 2034 Games. The Wasatch snow is famously dry and light, with more than ten ski resorts (Snowbird, Alta, Park City and others) within an hour's drive.",
    // howItWorks sources: en.wikipedia (2026-09, Tier B short): Salt Lake City (Utah; capital of Utah and seat of Salt Lake County; 199,723 in 2020, metro ~1.3 million, 46th-largest US metro; founded 1847 by settlers under Brigham Young, originally Great Salt Lake City until 1868; wide streets of 132 feet on a grid centred on Temple Square, from the Plat of the City of Zion; church headquarters at Temple Square; the Great Salt Lake receding, exposing arsenic; Wasatch ski resorts; 2002 Winter Olympics and 2034 awarded in July 2024; SLC airport hub; University of Utah)
    howItWorks:
      "Salt Lake City is the capital of Utah and the seat of Salt Lake County, with about 218,000 people in the 2025 estimate and a metropolitan area (officially the Salt Lake City-Murray MSA) of about 1.3 million; the city council runs local services and the county and state the rest. Settlers of the Latter-day Saints under Brigham Young arrived in 1847, and the city was laid out at once on the Plat of the City of Zion: streets forty metres wide, and addresses counted from Temple Square as origin in the form of direction plus block number, so that every address states how many blocks it stands from the centre — a system still in use and the first thing a newcomer must learn. The church headquarters and temple stand on the square and the University of Utah at the foot of the hills to the east. Mountains ring the valley: the Wasatch ski resorts are within an hour, the Winter Olympics were held here in 2002 and awarded again for 2034; the Great Salt Lake to the west keeps falling, and dust from its exposed bed is a local environmental problem, while winter inversions foul the air in the basin. Newcomers most often go wrong by reading addresses as in an ordinary city, when here they are a set of coordinates.",
    layout:
      "The city is a large grid with Temple Square as its origin, streets numbered by their distance from the square (300 South and so on); the State Capitol stands on the hillside north of downtown. Downtown holds the convention centre, the City Creek shopping centre and historic blocks; the University of Utah and the Natural History Museum are at the foot of the mountains to the east. The Wasatch Range rises to the east, with Big and Little Cottonwood canyons leading to the ski resorts (about 40 minutes) and Park City about 50 km away on the far side; the Great Salt Lake lies north-west, with Antelope Island State Park the best shoreline viewpoint about 70 km away. The airport is about 6 km west of downtown.",
    gettingAround:
      "Salt Lake City International Airport (SLC), about 6 km from downtown, is a Delta hub linked by the TRAX Green Line light rail to the centre. Amtrak's California Zephyr calls daily. The city has three TRAX lines, a streetcar and buses; downtown works on foot and by rail; UTA ski buses run to the resorts in winter, otherwise a car is needed. The climate is semi-arid continental with hot dry summers and cold snowy winters (about 1.5 m of snow a year) and winter inversion haze.",
    culture:
      "English is universal. Temple Square is the heart of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints: the visitor centres and gardens are open with volunteer missionary guides, the temple itself is closed to visitors — dress modestly and keep quiet. Utah's alcohol rules differ from other states (regulated bar and restaurant service; spirits sold only in state stores). Tip 15–20% in restaurants. Many shops and restaurants close on Sundays. Outdoor culture is intense; backcountry travel in avalanche season needs proper gear and information.",
    seeAndDo:
      "Temple Square with the Church History Museum and the Family History Library; the State Capitol; the Natural History Museum of Utah; hiking in Red Butte Canyon and the Wasatch (summer); skiing at Snowbird, Alta, Brighton and Park City (November–April); Park City's old town and the Olympic park; the Great Salt Lake and bison on Antelope Island; the Bonneville Salt Flats (about 190 km west); the dining districts of 9th & 9th and Sugar House.",
    whenAndTips:
      "December–March is ski season (best snow in January–February), June–September for hiking and climbing; spring brings snowmelt and autumn golden aspens. A day for the city, one or two for skiing or Park City. Canyon roads often require four-wheel drive or chains in winter. Salt Lake City is the starting point for drives to Utah's five national parks (Zion, Bryce and the rest).",
